                    CSIRACEM - A CSIRAC EMULATOR
                   ----------------------------

 This program is designed to emulate the CSIRAC instruction set on
an IBM-compatible PC. It makes no attempt to mimic the architecture or speed
of CSIRAC nor to reproduce any of its known idiosyncracies, many of
which in any case were eliminated during the time of my use of the
machine (1959-1964). It does however strive to emulate most of the
features of the CSIRAC console and displays, within the limits of
a text screen under DOS 6.2. A more realistic display may be possible
by using a graphics screen, but that will be left for later consideration.
The program is written in TurboPascal version 6, using the lower
20 bits of the 32 bit Longint type to hold CSIRAC words.

The CSIRAC program to be run is simulated by a text file, which normally
starts with a Primary routine (without X-punches), a control routine,
the subroutines used by the main program, then the main program itself.
The Primary is first read, then control is passed to it by clearing the
Sequence register to allow the remainder of the program to be read
into memory under control of the primary. This operation is automatic
in the CSIRACEM program, and no manual intervention (such as to set NA or
clear the sequence register) is necessary, unless some non-standard primary
such as Muncey's non-stop primary is being used, when NA must be set
before the program is read in (see later).
Because the end of the primary is signalled by the occurrence of
blank tape, some blank rows must be left after the primary when
the program is assembled, as was normal practice with CSIRAC.
The stored program may then be executed.
Output is written to text files (one each for the OT and OP destinations).
The content of these files is the text in ASCII of what would appear on the
Teleprinter (for OT) or what would appear when the output tape was
printed by the Flexowriter (for OP with 5-hole punch selected). When the
5-hole punch is selected, output is character by character; when the
12-hole punch is selected output is row by row and each tape row is
output in the same format as the input format described below.

Program Input Format
--------------------
columns
1234567
mm nnXY  where mm and nn are the most and least significant 32-scale
         numbers, X = X if there is an X punch, else space,
                  Y = Y if there is a Y punch, else space.

A program is read from a text file, in a format where each tape row
is represented by two 32-scale numbers separated by a space and denoting
either the most significant and least significant parts of the address
or the 32-scale equivalent of the source and destination. This occupies
5 columns. In addition, if the row contains an X-punch there is an
X in column 6; if the row contains a Y-punch there is a Y in column 7.
Thus the command 31 26 K PS (occupying two tape rows) would be
represented by
31 26^^
26 24X^
and the control statement 4A (also occupying two tape rows) would be
represented by
 0  4^^
 0  2^Y
where ^ represents a space character.
Any information in columns >7 in the input file is ignored, and may
be used for comments.
The program file may be headed by one or more lines (starting beyond
column 7) of heading, terminated by a blank row ( with ^0^^0^^ in
columns 1-7 where ^ represents a space). Several more blank rows normally
follow before the program starts, and it is usual to have several
blank rows at the end of the file.
Program files are normally named with an extension of .CVT, but this
is not mandatory.

Data Input/Output
-----------------
Data input will also be from a text file, the format of which is determined
by the input routine used and the program using it. Only one data file
which may be for the 5-hole or the 12-hole reader is currently
allowed for. 
5-hole data are read in ASCII, character by character not row by row,
so that the 5-hole data file may be in any convenient layout, usually
in the form resulting when a 5-hole data tape was printed out on the
Flexowriter. ASCII characters are automatically converted to
Flexowriter code on input and are used internally in that form, so that 
subroutines for handling Flexowriter code work as they used to. The
internal Flexowriter code is automatically converted back to ASCII on
output. 
If it is necessary to specifically indicate the shift status in an input
file (as sometimes required in Interprogram data files), figure shift and 
letter shift may be represented by the lower case letters f and s 
respectively, and a blank tape row may be indicated by a lower case b.
Blank rows specifically output are represented in the output by a lower 
case b and the Flexowriter stop code by a lower case s.
Thus 5-hole data files are prepared in the ASCII code used by
the IBM-compatible PC, and 5-hole output files are also in ASCII, and 
are thus immediately readable by the PC.
Data output to the Teleprinter (destination OT) are also automatically
converted to ASCII in a file readable by the PC.
12-hole data are read row by row. 12-hole data tapes originally output
from CSIRAC, when converted to the m nXY format by the tape reader 
in the Melbourne University Computer Science Department, may be read
by the program used with CSIRAC to read it, when the program has been 
converted in the same way.

Invoking the Emulator Program
-----------------------------
The program is invoked by entering

CSIRACEM ProgramFileName DataFileName

on the command line from DOS or from a DOS box in Windows 3.x or 95.
With Windows 95 or 98 it may be better to run the program in MSDOS mode.
Steps are being taken to create a 32-bit version using Free Pascal instead
of TurboPascal, which it is expected will run in a DOS box in
Windows 95 or 98.
ProgramFileName and DataFileName may be any valid DOS filenames, and
should include the path if they are located in a different directory
to that from which the program is invoked.  A file MAHOLD, which
holds the contents of the drum store MA, is written to the current
directory (or to the directory where the CSIRACEM.EXE file resides if
a path is included) on exit if any writes to MA have been encountered
during program execution. This file (if it exists) is automatically
read each time a program is launched, thus preserving the drum contents.
Inclusion of the filenames on the command line is optional, and if
not present they are requested when the program is loaded. If no
data file is required, just RETURN when the data filename is
requested, and no data file will be expected. The program stops
with an appropriate error message if the designated files cannot
be located.
Output filenames are automatically generated from the Program
filename by deleting any extension and replacing it with OT
for the Teleprinter output file, or OP for the Flexowriter or 
12-hole output file. 
Any output sent to the Teleprinter, as well as being written to the
.OT file, is echoed in a window below the D register display. This is
of necessity e.g. to enable instructions from Interprogram to be seen as they
are sent, as it would be impractical to have to examine the .OT file.
On exit from the program any zero length files (i.e. when the OT and/or OP 
destination is not used by the program) are deleted.
After valid filename(s) have been entered and their existence checked,
a screen of information appears, giving the initial Control Desk Switch 
Panel settings assumed, and the default initial state of registers, 
Memory and Drum store:
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The initial Control Desk Switch Panel settings are:                             
                                                                                
Unit add to S per command: ON                                                   
Reader Switch: ON   Reader Selector: 12 HOLE                                    
Halt Selector: OFF  Speed Selector: HIGH                                        
Punch: ON   Printer: ON   Punch Selector: 5 HOLE                                
Main store display selector: 0  0 (to display primary usually)                  
Display: CONTENT OF ALL D REGISTERS                                             
Halt Selector address: 23  31                                                   
NA register: 0 0 0 0  NB register: 0 0 0 0  I register: 0 0 0 0                 
Clearance Buttons: (All Reg,A,B,C,All D,H,S,I,Out,Tape Reader): OFF             
One Shot: OFF                                                                   
NA & S to K: OFF   NA to K: OFF                                                 
Drum Writing Control: ON                                                        
Start/Stop: START  Start: ON                                                    
                                                                                
Machine starts with (S)=0, All Registers, Main Store clear                      
Auxiliary (drum) store not cleared                                              
                                                                                
Video display on during program execution                                       
                                                                                
                                                                                
                                                                                
                                                                                
Press O for options or RETURN to read program into memory                       
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

If O is selected another screen appears listing the alterable
parameters and an Options menu:

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The current settings are:                                                       
Reader Selector: 12 HOLE    Punch Selector: 5 HOLE      NA to K: OFF            
NA register:  0  0  0  0    NB register:  0  0  0  0    I register:  0  0  0  0 
One-Shot switch: OFF   Halt Selector: OFF    Halt Selector address:  0  0       
Memory Display Address:  0  0   Display D registers                             
Video ON during program execution                                               
Press F to read from 12-hole data File instead of program file                  
      R to Read in another program                                              
      N to toggle NA to K switch (default OFF)                                  
      P to load a T001 Primary and Control stored in memory into 0 0 to 0 24    
      5 to toggle punch between 5 hole (default) and 12 hole                    
      E to Edit memory                                                          
      M to set Memory display address (default 0 0)                             
      T to TSP a section of memory                                              
      A to set NA register, B to set NB register, I to set I register           
      S to clear Sequence register                                              
      H to set Halt Selector and/or Halt Selector address                       
      L to Look at register and memory display then return here                 
      O to toggle OneShot switch (default OFF)                                  
      C to toggle display between 0s and 1s (default) and "more like CSIRAC"    
      D to toggle display between D registers and last 16 commands executed     
      V to disable video during program execution                               
      X to eXit (return to Operating System)                                    
or RETURN to accept above settings                                              
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Notes
. F is for changing between reading the 12-hole program file and reading
   a 12-hole data file and is equivalent to removing the program tape
   from the reader, and inserting a 12-hole data tape. This is
   necessary to let the CSIRACEM program know that the file will
   be read as data under control of the stored program, and not
   interpreted as program and stored in memory.
. R is for Reading in another program, which may or may not
   include a primary. The Sequence Register is automatically cleared in
   this operation.
. P is for storing a standard Primary and control (for use if a program
   lacking a primary and control is to be input). The program to follow must
   start with a transfer control statement in this case.
. T is for producing a Tape Symbol Print (TSP) of memory or drum between
   designated addresses.
. O is to set the One-shot switch or H the halt selector which stops
   the program after the current or selected command is executed.
. L is for Looking at the register and memory display without altering the
   sequence register, and then returning to the Options menu. This
   option is useful to inspect the display after changing the memory
   display start address or after changing between D register
   and last 16 commands executed display, without causing execution 
   of the program to start or continue.
. D is for toggling between a display of the D registers or of the last 16
   commands executed. The record of the last 16 commands executed
   is continually updated, but is only displayed when execution is
   halted (if its display has been selected).  It is unreadable when
   a program is executing anyway.
. V may be used to disable the register display during program execution.
    Refreshing the screen display is time-consuming, and selecting V can
    significantly reduce the running time of a program which would
    normally run for several minutes. It is not necessary to use this
    option except with long-running programs.
. X is for eXiting to the Operating System with an optional display of
   the last screenful of output. No provision is made to move through
   the output file - if a more complete examination is required, look at
   the ProgramName.OT file or the ProgramName.OP file with a text reader.
   This option is the normal way of concluding a program.
Though upper case letters are specified in the menu, lower case letters
are acceptable. The Options menu itself can be entered by pressing
o instead of O if convenient.
Several parameters may be altered; as each change is completed the
current settings display at the top is altered to reflect the change made. 
It is important always to check that the requisite (5-hole or 12-hole)
reader and punch are selected, as the wrong combination  will
produce nonsense or an error message.
Note that Muncey's non-stop primary requires that NA be set to 2 0 1 0 
BEFORE reading in the program.
When all parameters are as required, pressing RETURN will start 
program reading.
When the program is read in, and the final DO command leads to a stop in
the normal manner, another opportunity is available to select the Options
menu, to toggle the reader or to press RETURN to start program execution. 
A typical operating screen shows:
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
A 00000 00000 00000 00000   B 01011 00101 00101 10100                       *  *
C 01010 01110 01101 00110   H 10110 00001     O 00001      K 00000 00000    S  T
S 01010 01110 00000 00000  MA 01011 00110 11011 00000                           
I 11000 00000 11111 10000                                                       
            D                     M (0,0) onwards                               
  10000 00001 10101 01110    00000 10101 11010 10111   READER: 12 HOLE          
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00000 10001 10010                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00000 10010 11001                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00110 11010 11000                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 01100 10101 00101                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 01101 01011 11000                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 01110 01110 11010                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00000 01001 00000                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00001 11000 01111                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00000 11000 11000                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00011 10110 00101                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00000 00001 10001                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00000 10001 10101                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00000 10010 11001                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00111 11000 10111                            
  00000 00000 00000 00000    00000 00110 10110 11000                            
                                                                                
                                                                                
                                                                                
Press O for options, R to toggle reader or RETURN to execute program            
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The top line of this screen shows the content of the A and B registers,
in each case as 4 groups of 5 binary digits.
In all cases of 20 bit registers, the p20 digit is at the left and
the p1 digit at the right hand end.
The second line shows the 20-bit content of the C register, the 10 digits of
the half-register H, the logical sum of the p15-p11 and p5-p1 digits of
the Output register O, the p20-p11 digits of the Interpreter (K) register,
and finally the source and destination mnemonics of the last instruction
executed.
The third line shows the 20-bit contents of the Sequence register S, and
the 20 digits of the last number sent to the drum (MA).
The fourth line shows the 20-bit contents of the Input register I.
Below are shown on the left the content of the 16 D registers (or the
20-bit representation of the last 16 commands executed, if this has been
selected) then the contents of 16 successive memory (M) cells, starting
at the designated address. To the right of these columns is an indicator
of which reader (5-hole or 12-hole) is currently selected.
This display includes most of the information available from the
CRT displays and indicator lights observable from the operator console
position.
At any time during program execution, pressing any key will pause
the running program to enable examination of a static display, and
to change Options by pressing O, or to continue execution by pressing
RETURN.
By selecting O and then X from the Options menu the CSIRACEM program
may be terminated, and control returned to the operating system.
An emergency exit from the CSIRACEM program is usually available by
hitting Control/Break twice, but this would cause some output to be lost.
When One-Shot is on, or when the program is halted on reaching a
selected Halt address, a two line display of the decimal content
of the A, B, C, D0, and D1 registers appears near the bottom of
the screen. An example is shown below:  

Fraction  A -0.003456  B -0.120117  C 0.524288  D0 -0.937487  D1 0.000000       
Integer   A    -1812  B   -62976  C   274878  D0  -491513   D1        0         

The top line shows the register content as a fraction (binary
point between p20 and p19) and the bottom line shows the same
content as an integer (binary point to the right of p1). I hope
that this is not too offensive to purists, but it can be very
useful.

Error Messages
--------------
 The following error messages may be encountered when using the CSIRACEM
 program:

 1. "Filename" does not exist. Try again.

 This message occurs when the specified program or data file "Filename"
 cannot be  located. It may be due to misspelling, wrong filename extension
 (normally .CVT) etc. It may also occur because the file is not in the
 directory from which the CSIRACEM program was launched. In this case the
 full DOS path must be supplied as "Filename".
 Two further tries may be made to correct the situation; if unsuccessful
 the message

 3 tries and you're out

 appears and the CSIRACEM program terminates.

 2. Error on line n

 This message can occur when the primary, the program, or a 12-hole
 data file is being read in if one or both of the 32-scale numbers on
 line n is out of range, or the line is otherwise invalidly formatted.
 The CSIRACEM program terminates after this message so that the error can
 be corrected.

 3. Error(s) in input data. Please try again.

 This message arises from the operations under the Options menu when
 input of 2 or 4 32-scale numbers is asked for, and the numbers are out of
 the required range, or not correctly spaced. As many tries as necessary
 may be made until valid input is attained. The message may occur with the
 I, A, B, E, H, M or T options.

 4. Unexpected EOF on 5-hole data file

 This message occurs when an attempt is made to read past the
 end-of-file on the file representing a 5-hole data tape. It may
 be caused by having the wrong reader selected.It is a fatal error 
 and causes termination of the CSIRACEM program.

 5. Unexpected End of File - probably missing DO
    or no blank rows at end of tape

 This message is encountered when an end-of-file is found when reading
 the file representing the CSIRAC program. It is usually due to the
 requisite DO command being omitted from the end of the program file,
 or may result when the wrong reader is selected, and causes 
 the CSIRACEM program to terminate.

 Every effort has been made to avoid the occurrence of error messages
 generated from the Pascal compiler, which are likely to be unintelligible.
 Please advise me if you get such a message, and if full details of
 the circumstances are provided, an attempt will be made to correct
 the situation.

Limitations
-----------
. The O register display shows only the lower 5 digits, made up of
  the disjunction of p15..p11 with p5..p1, and represents the character
  sent to destination OT or OP when the 5-hole punch is selected,
  but only represents the lower 5 digits of the row sent when the
  12-hole punch is selected.
. Only one data file, which may be for 5-hole or 12-hole input is allowed for.
. Only one pair of output files (ProgramName.OT and ProgramName.OP)
  is allowed for.
  If more than one program is executed, output from subsequent
  program(s) is appended to the existing output file(s), under the name of
  the original program, even if a different output punch was selected.
. Three Greek Teleprinter characters cannot be represented from the English
  code page 437 and are replaced by spaces.
. The changed left shift coding introduced with the fast optimizing
  mode 3 on CSIRAC is not supported. 
These limitations are not absolute, and most could be overcome by
modifying the program if it is found necessary to do so.

Reports of any errors found in the CSIRACEM program will be dolefully
received. Any criticisms and suggestions will be gratefully received.

